To securely manage secrets in Terraform, you must prevent them from being hardcoded in your configuration, exposed in logs, or left unprotected in state files. [1, 2]
Core Strategies for Secret Management
• Mark Variables as Sensitive: Use the sensitive = true argument in variable and output blocks. This redacts the values from Terraform CLI logs and console output, though they remain stored in the state file.
• Use Ephemeral Values: For Terraform 1.10+, you can use the ephemeral argument on variables to prevent sensitive data from ever being stored in state or plan files.
• External Secret Managers: Instead of passing secrets as variables, fetch them dynamically at runtime using data sources from dedicated tools like HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or Azure Key Vault.
• Secure Remote Backends: Since secrets often end up in the state file, store it in a remote backend (like AWS S3 or Azure Blob Storage) with encryption at rest and strict access controls (IAM). [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
“Terraform configuration files consist of core blocks like provider, resource, variables, outputs, locals, data sources, modules, and the terraform block. These components together define, parameterize, and manage infrastructure as code.”



• terraform plan → full diff (config vs actual infra)
• terraform plan -refresh-only → drift check only



🔹 Variables
	• Defined by the user or passed into a function
	• Can change during execution
	• Used to store data dynamically

🔹 Locals (Local Variables)
• Variables inside a specific function or block
• Only accessible within that scope




